xample: (A,7) -> (C,5).\n\nC5 is column C, row5.\n\nAdjacent squares: B4, B5, B6, C4, C6, D4, D5, D6.\n\nB4 is opponent\'s piece, so moving to C5 would flip B4.\n\nLet\'s check:\n\nFrom A7 to C5: dx=+2 (A to C), dy=-2 (7 to5). Max 2, valid jump.\n\nYes, valid action.\n\nAdjacent to C5 is B4 (C5\'s left-down is B4? Let\'s see:\n\nC5 is column3, row5.\n\nB4 is column2, row4.\n\nSo dx= -1, dy= -1 from C5 to B4. So yes, adjacent (diagonally).\n\nSo placing at C5 would flip B4.\n\nSimilarly, this would flip B4.\n\nSo both B5 and C5 moves from A7 would flip B4.\n\nAre there other opponent pieces that can be flipped?\n\nA3 is at (A,3). To flip A3, need to move to adjacent square.\n\nAdjacent squares to A3 are A2, A4, B2, B3, B4.\n\nSo if I can move to any of those, A3 would flip.\n\nFor example, moving to B4 would flip A3 (since B4 is adjacent to A3).\n\nBut B4 is currently an opponent piece, so I can\'t move there unless I capture it via flipping first.\n\nAlternatively, moving to B3.\n\nIs there a valid move to B3?\n\nFrom where?\n\nLooking at my pieces:\n\nA7 could potentially move to B5, C5, etc., but B3 is row3.\n\nFrom A7 to B3: dx=+1, dy=-4. Way too far.\n\nFrom F4: F4 to B3 would be dx=-4, dy=-1. No.\n\nFrom G5: G5 to B3 is dx=-5, dy=-2. No.\n\nFrom G1: G1 to B3 is dx=-5, dy=+2. No.\n\nSo no, can\'t reach B3.\n\nHow about moving to A4?\n\nA4 is adjacent to A3.\n\nFrom A7 to A4: dy=-3, which is more than 2. Not valid.\n\nSo probably can\'t flip A3 right now.\n\nSo the only opponent piece that can be flipped is B4, via moving to B5 or C5 or other adjacent squares.\n\nLet\'s check all possible moves that can flip B4.\n\nB4 is at (B,4).\n\nAdjacent squares are:\n\nA3 (opponent), A4, A5,\n\nB3, B5,\n\nC3, C4, C5.\n\nSo any move to A4, A5, B3, B5, C3, C4, C5 would be adjacent to B4 and flip it.\n\nNow, check if any of my pieces can move to those squares.\n\nLet\'s see:\n\nA4: Can I move there?\n\nFrom A7: A7 to A4 is dy=-3 (7-4=3), which is more than 2. Not valid.\n\nFro